.TH "tangsible-rerun" "1" "2026-09-05" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
tangsible-rerun - repeat the last playbook run
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBtangsible rerun\fR [\fIplaybook.\&yml or role name\fR] [--start-at-play \fIname\fR] [\fIansible-playbook args\fR .\&.\&.\&]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBrerun\fR will repeat the last playbook run.\& Before the rerun is started, the
user can modify certain settings (e.\&g.\& hosts, tags, etc.\&) of the previous
run.\& Confirming the dialog starts the run exactly as \fBtangsible-run\fR(1)
would have.\&
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
With no target (\fIplaybook.\&yml\fR or \fIrole\fR) given at all, \fBrerun\fR falls
back to whichever playbook or role was invoked most recently, by any
verb.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Otherwise the last invocation of the specified \fIplaybook.\&yml\fR or \fIrole\fR
will be rerun.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Explicit \fB--tags\fR/\fB-t\fR, \fB--skip-tags\fR, \fB--limit\fR/\fB-l\fR, or
\fB--start-at-play\fR if given, win over that target'\&s own history — otherwise the
history entry'\&s own tags/skip-tags/hosts pre-fill the dialog.\&
.IP \(bu 4
Any \fIother\fR passthrough argument given on the command line (inventory,
extra-vars, verbosity, .\&.\&.\&) replaces the history entry'\&s own remembered
arguments outright, rather than merging with them.\&
.PD
.PP
A target with no recorded history at all (never run before) still works —
every dialog field simply starts empty.\&
.PP
The rerun dialog itself offers options for \fBStart with play\fR, \fBStart with
task\fR, \fBLimit tags\fR, \fBSkip tags\fR, and \fBLimit hosts\fR - each corresponding to
the respective options.\&
.PP
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
Same as \fBtangsible-run\fR(1); see \fBtangsible\fR(1).\& Resolving no target at all
(see above) is a usage error.\&
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBtangsible\fR(1), \fBtangsible-run\fR(1), \fBtangsible-role\fR(1)
